diff --git a/docs/subsystems/gateway-routing.md b/docs/subsystems/gateway-routing.md
index 093a5b4ed..5286bd707 100644
--- a/docs/subsystems/gateway-routing.md
+++ b/docs/subsystems/gateway-routing.md
@@ -243,6 +243,7 @@ recorded as a prediction failure (`noPrediction`).
 - Codex offers its image tool (the `image_gen` namespace, #870) to every magpie provider. When a provider other than a ChatGPT sign-in or openai.com answers a bare 400, `attempt` asks it once more without the tool (`image_tool.go`). This happens both passed through and translated. If that works, the provider is marked unfit for the tool (`tool:image_gen`) and isn't offered it again. If it doesn't, the tool is restored and the 400 stands (#949).
 - A turn the agent stopped with its tool call still out is replayed by the client with the call and no result, and Google's APIs turn a history whose call turn goes unanswered away with 400, every later turn of the conversation with it. The Gemini build (`buildCodeAssistSent`, which serves a Google sign-in, Vertex AI and a custom provider's Gemini API alike) answers such a call with the synthetic result the chat build pairs an interrupted turn's call with (`pairToolMessages`, #1275), right after the calls (`pairFunctionTurns`, #1560): beside the answers the client sent there, or in a user turn of its own before what the user said, since a turn mixing a `functionResponse` with text is turned away too (#1533); a Claude model on Antigravity gets it in the one tool_result turn. A call with no id counts as answered by a response of its name. A second answer to one call is left out, with the images it carried, before the turns are built (`dropSecondAnswers`, #2262): Google refuses a call turn whose `functionResponse` count differs from its `functionCall` count, and the Chat and Messages builds already keep the first answer. The Responses build (`buildResponses`) leaves it out the same way. A call made again under the same id takes an answer of its own. A Gemini client that keeps no ids answers two calls of one function by name in their order, and each answer goes to its own call (`parseGemini`), not both to the latest. `TestGeminiDropsASecondAnswer`, `TestSecondAnswerDroppedWithItsImages`, `TestGeminiClientUnnamedCallsAnsweredInOrder`.
 - A request built for a Messages upstream (`buildAnthropic`: a Chat, Responses or Gemini client translated) has its tool exchanges mended after its messages are built (`pairToolBlocks`, #1558, ggbdpq), since Anthropic answers 400 for a `tool_use` the next message doesn't answer and for a `tool_result` naming no call of the message before it. A call left unanswered gets the synthetic interrupted result, `is_error` set, first in the user message after it or in one of its own; a result that answers no call goes as a text block with what it said (its images as they came); a second answer to one call is dropped. A well-formed exchange goes as it was built. An Anthropic client relayed to a Messages upstream isn't mended. [`anthropic_tool_pairing_test.go`](../../internal/gateway/anthropic_tool_pairing_test.go).
+- A Messages request relayed or translated to a Messages upstream that wants a turn's thinking back (`anthropicReplaysThinking`, the gate of `withThinkingOnToolTurns` above) has a parallel tool exchange — one assistant message with several `tool_use` blocks answered by the `tool_result` blocks of the user message after it, the shape Claude Code sends for parallel tool calls — split into the sequential exchanges of one call each (`splitParallelToolUses` in [`anthropic.go`](../../internal/gateway/anthropic.go)): DeepSeek's Anthropic-format API alone refuses the parallel one however its results are sent, 400 "`tool_use` ids were found without `tool_result` blocks immediately after" (deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V3#967, with the raw SDK there), although Anthropic's own API takes it, so sequential tool calls worked and parallel ones failed every turn after (#4945). What the user said beside the results stays after the last of them; an exchange of one call, or one the next message doesn't fully answer, goes as it was. `TestMessagesParallelToolUsesSplitForDeepSeekUpstream`, `TestSplitParallelToolUsesOnARelayedBody`.
 - A Responses request bound for anything but a ChatGPT sign-in has its tool exchanges mended in `attempt`, before it is relayed or translated, plugins included ([`responses.go`](../../internal/gateway/responses.go)). An item inside a run of calls and results (after one of the run's results and before the last, or before any result when the model didn't write it) moves to the end of that run (`joinToolRuns`, #1752, Edward4226): Codex writes its `<image_resize_notice>` right after each result whose image it resized, so a batch of two calls had it between their results, and a strict upstream answered 400 (OpenCode Go/DeepSeek "No tool output found for tool call …", Volcengine's "must be followed by tool messages", #1822); a Chat translation answered the second call with a synthetic result. An item carrying a `call_id` never moves, the model's own reasoning between its calls stays, and its text between them (an assistant message, with the reasoning right before it) goes ahead of the run's first call, so the step's calls stand together: OpenCode Go opens a new assistant message at such text and answered the call before it "No tool output found for tool call …" (#2100). A call opens a run only when a result after it answers it. A result with no call id becomes a user message after its run (`orphanedToolOutputs`), and a call nothing answers gets a synthetic result at the end of its run (`pairToolItems`, #1341). `TestRelayedCodexImageBatchKeepsItsResultsTogether`, `TestJoinToolRunsOnlyMovesWhatBreaksARun`, `TestRelayedTextBetweenABatchsCallsIsAnswered`.
 - A Chat client's own body relayed to a Chat upstream (`passthrough`) has its tool exchanges mended when they don't pair (`toolPairsOut` in [`chattidy.go`](../../internal/gateway/chattidy.go)), by the rule a translated one is built with (`pairToolMessages` in [`chat.go`](../../internal/gateway/chat.go)): Kimi's Chat API, like OpenAI's, turns the whole request away for an assistant's call the tool messages right after it don't answer ("an assistant message with 'tool_calls' must be followed by tool messages responding to each 'tool_call_id'…"), a user message between a call and its answer included, and for a tool message with no call before it ("tool_call_id  is not found"), both checked against api.kimi.com/coding on 2026-10-26. Qoder's chat forked at an earlier turn, its model switched to Kimi Code, was refused on every prompt (Paidaixinbao on X). A turn's calls split over consecutive assistant messages join into one first (`joinSplitChatCalls`); a call left unanswered gets the synthetic interrupted result; an answer to no call becomes the user's text, a parts content by its text parts. A body whose exchange pairs goes byte for byte, answers out of the calls' order included, which Kimi takes (`TestChatRelayPairsToolsForKimi`, `TestChatRelayPairedToolsUntouched`).
 - A Chat request has every tool call that came with no id (`""`, null or none) given one in `attempt`, before it is relayed or translated, plugins included (`chatCallIDs` in [`chat_call_ids.go`](../../internal/gateway/chat_call_ids.go), #2611, RunMintOn). An upstream that sometimes streams a call without an id (OpenCode Zen's free DeepSeek) has the agent keep it as `""`, which that upstream takes back, while WorkBuddy AI turns a streamed request carrying one away (400, code 11133) and Anthropic takes no empty `tool_use` id. The id is made from the call's place in the request, its name and its arguments, never at random, so the next turn's history goes byte for byte and the upstream's prompt cache holds; the tool messages after the call that answer with no `tool_call_id` take the made ids in the calls' order. A body whose calls all have ids goes as it came. On the reply side, a relayed Chat stream's call whose first fragment has no id is given one, and a later fragment's empty id is left out (`chatTidy`); a whole reply's call is given one too (`chatWhole`). `TestEmptyCallIDRelayedWithOne`, `TestEmptyCallIDReachesPluginWithOne`, `TestEmptyCallIDTranslatedWithOne`, `TestStreamedCallGivenAnID` in [`chat_call_ids_test.go`](../../internal/gateway/chat_call_ids_test.go).
diff --git a/internal/gateway/anthropic.go b/internal/gateway/anthropic.go
index afe258b09..4392b3ff8 100644
--- a/internal/gateway/anthropic.go
+++ b/internal/gateway/anthropic.go
@@ -418,6 +418,146 @@ func withThinkingOnToolTurns(body []byte) []byte {
 	return nb
 }
 
+// splitParallelToolUses turns a parallel tool exchange of a Messages
+// request — one assistant message with several tool_use blocks, its results
+// the tool_result blocks of the user message after it, the shape Claude
+// Code sends for parallel tool calls — into the sequential exchanges
+// DeepSeek's Anthropic-format API alone answers: one call an assistant
+// message, its result the user message after it. It refuses the parallel
+// one however the results are sent ("tool_use ids were found without
+// tool_result blocks immediately after", deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V3#967,
+// checked with the raw SDK there), although Anthropic's own API takes it
+// (#4945). What the user said beside the results stays in the last
+// exchange's user message, after its result. An exchange of one call, or
+// one the next message doesn't fully answer, goes as it was, and a body
+// with nothing to split comes back byte for byte.
+func splitParallelToolUses(body []byte) []byte {
+	if !bytes.Contains(body, []byte(`"tool_use"`)) {
+		return body
+	}
+	var q map[string]json.RawMessage
+	var msgs []json.RawMessage
+	if decodeJSON(body, &q) != nil || decodeJSON(q["messages"], &msgs) != nil {
+		return body
+	}
+	blockIDs := func(m json.RawMessage) (role string, blocks []json.RawMessage) {
+		var msg struct {
+			Role    string            `json:"role"`
+			Content []json.RawMessage `json:"content"`
+		}
+		if decodeJSON(m, &msg) != nil {
+			return "", nil
+		}
+		return msg.Role, msg.Content
+	}
+	message := func(role string, blocks []json.RawMessage) json.RawMessage {
+		b, _ := json.Marshal(map[string]any{"role": role, "content": blocks})
+		return b
+	}
+	var out []json.RawMessage
+	changed := false
+	for i := 0; i < len(msgs); i++ {
+		role, blocks := blockIDs(msgs[i])
+		var calls []json.RawMessage
+		if role == "assistant" {
+			for _, b := range blocks {
+				var t struct {
+					Type string `json:"type"`
+				}
+				if decodeJSON(b, &t) == nil && t.Type == "tool_use" {
+					calls = append(calls, b)
+				}
+			}
+		}
+		if len(calls) < 2 || i+1 >= len(msgs) {
+			out = append(out, msgs[i])
+			continue
+		}
+		// the calls' ids, and their results in the user message after
+		ids := make([]string, len(calls))
+		for k, c := range calls {
+			var call struct {
+				ID string `json:"id"`
+			}
+			decodeJSON(c, &call)
+			ids[k] = call.ID
+		}
+		nextRole, results := blockIDs(msgs[i+1])
+		if nextRole != "user" {
+			out = append(out, msgs[i])
+			continue
+		}
+		byID := map[string]json.RawMessage{}
+		var rest []json.RawMessage
+		paired := true
+		for _, b := range results {
+			var r struct {
+				Type      string `json:"type"`
+				ToolUseID string `json:"tool_use_id"`
+			}
+			if decodeJSON(b, &r) != nil || r.Type != "tool_result" {
+				rest = append(rest, b)
+				continue
+			}
+			if _, answered := byID[r.ToolUseID]; answered || !slices.Contains(ids, r.ToolUseID) {
+				paired = false // answered twice, or answers no call of this exchange
+				break
+			}
+			byID[r.ToolUseID] = b
+		}
+		for _, id := range ids {
+			if byID[id] == nil {
+				paired = false // a call with no result: another mend's business
+			}
+		}
+		if !paired {
+			out = append(out, msgs[i])
+			continue
+		}
+		// the exchange split: each call an assistant message of its own,
+		// its result the user message after it, what the user said beside
+		// them after the last result
+		var first []json.RawMessage
+		seen := 0
+		for _, b := range blocks {
+			var t struct {
+				Type string `json:"type"`
+			}
+			decodeJSON(b, &t)
+			if t.Type == "tool_use" {
+				if seen > 0 {
+					continue
+				}
+				seen++
+			}
+			first = append(first, b)
+		}
+		out = append(out, message("assistant", first))
+		for k, id := range ids {
+			if k > 0 {
+				out = append(out, message("assistant", []json.RawMessage{calls[k]}))
+			}
+			last := k == len(ids)-1
+			content := []json.RawMessage{byID[id]}
+			if last && len(rest) > 0 {
+				content = append(content, rest...)
+			}
+			out = append(out, message("user", content))
+		}
+		changed = true
+		i++ // the user message of the exchange went with it
+	}
+	if !changed {
+		return body
+	}
+	q["messages"], _ = json.Marshal(out)
+	nb, err := marshalJSON(q)
+	if err != nil {
+		return body
+	}
+	return nb
+}
+
 // anthropicModel is one of Anthropic's own models, which think only when asked,
 // so a request for one is left as the agent sent it.
 var anthropicModel = regexp.MustCompile(`(?i)(?:^|[/.:-])claude-`)
diff --git a/internal/gateway/anthropic_tool_pairing_test.go b/internal/gateway/anthropic_tool_pairing_test.go
index 06e5487af..e7409ff56 100644
--- a/internal/gateway/anthropic_tool_pairing_test.go
+++ b/internal/gateway/anthropic_tool_pairing_test.go
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@ package gateway
 
 import (
 	"encoding/json"
+	"net/http/httptest"
 	"strconv"
 	"strings"
 	"testing"
@@ -35,7 +36,12 @@ type aMessageProbe struct {
 // e.g. "user assistant(A) user[A] user".
 func messagesTrace(t *testing.T, r *Request) (string, []aMessageProbe) {
 	t.Helper()
-	body := buildAnthropic(r, "claude-sonnet-4-5")
+	return traceOfMessages(t, buildAnthropic(r, "claude-sonnet-4-5"))
+}
+
+// traceOfMessages is messagesTrace of a request already built.
+func traceOfMessages(t *testing.T, body []byte) (string, []aMessageProbe) {
+	t.Helper()
 	var q struct {
 		Messages []aMessageProbe `json:"messages"`
 	}
@@ -343,3 +349,146 @@ func TestMessagesSecondAnswerDropped(t *testing.T) {
 		t.Fatalf("the first answer didn't stand: %s", got)
 	}
 }
+
+// refuseParallelToolUse refuses a Messages request the way DeepSeek's
+// Anthropic-format API refuses a parallel tool exchange: an assistant
+// message with two or more tool_use blocks is turned away even when the
+// next message answers every one of them (deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V3#967,
+// #4945), which Anthropic's own API takes.
+func refuseParallelToolUse(body []byte) (int, string) {
+	var q struct {
+		Messages []struct {
+			Content json.RawMessage `json:"content"`
+		} `json:"messages"`
+	}
+	if err := json.Unmarshal(body, &q); err != nil {
+		return 400, `{"type":"error","error":{"type":"invalid_request_error","message":"could not parse the request"}}`
+	}
+	for i, m := range q.Messages {
+		var blocks []aBlockProbe
+		if json.Unmarshal(m.Content, &blocks) != nil {
+			continue
+		}
+		var ids []string
+		for _, c := range blocks {
+			if c.Type == "tool_use" {
+				ids = append(ids, c.ID)
+			}
+		}
+		if len(ids) > 1 {
+			msg := "`tool_use` ids were found without `tool_result` blocks immediately after: " +
+				strings.Join(ids, ", ") + ". Each `tool_use` block must have a corresponding `tool_result` block in the next message."
+			return 400, messagesRefused(i, msg)
+		}
+	}
+	return 0, ""
+}
+
+// DeepSeek's Anthropic-format API turns a parallel tool exchange away —
+// one assistant message whose calls the next user message answers together,
+// the shape Claude Code sends for parallel tool calls — although
+// Anthropic's own API takes it (deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V3#967, checked
+// against it with the raw SDK there). A Messages request relayed to it is
+// asked in sequential exchanges instead: one call an assistant message,
+// its result the user message after it (#4945).
+func TestMessagesParallelToolUsesSplitForDeepSeekUpstream(t *testing.T) {
+	f := &fake{t: t, refuse: refuseParallelToolUse, reply: sse(
+		`data: {"type":"message_start","message":{"id":"m","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"x","content":[],"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":0}}}`,
+		`data: {"type":"content_block_start","index":0,"content_block":{"type":"text","text":""}}`,
+		`data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ok"}}`,
+		`data: {"type":"content_block_stop","index":0}`,
+		`data: {"type":"message_delta","delta":{"stop_reason":"end_turn"},"usage":{"output_tokens":1}}`,
+		`data: {"type":"message_stop"}`,
+	)}
+	noRests()
+	t.Cleanup(noRests)
+	t.Setenv("XDG_CONFIG_HOME", t.TempDir())
+	t.Setenv("XDG_CACHE_HOME", t.TempDir())
+	up := httptest.NewServer(f)
+	t.Cleanup(up.Close)
+	p := provider.Provider{ID: "fake", Name: "Fake", Key: "k", Models: []string{"deepseek-flash"}, Anthropic: up.URL}
+	if err := provider.Save(p); err != nil {
+		t.Fatal(err)
+	}
+	body := `{"model":"fake/deepseek-flash","stream":true,"max_tokens":64,` +
+		`"tools":[{"name":"Bash","description":"Run a command","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"command":{"type":"string"}}}}],` +
+		`"messages":[` +
+		`{"role":"user","content":"List the files twice."},` +
+		`{"role":"assistant","content":[` +
+		`{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I should run ls.","signature":"sig"},` +
+		`{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_00_YpfcrnrrW1pl5ZnBPhbn8836","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"ls"}},` +
+		`{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_01_CmdsQ2CAdkIsdARwuwAB4684","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"ls -a"}}]},` +
+		`{"role":"user","content":[` +
+		`{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_00_YpfcrnrrW1pl5ZnBPhbn8836","content":"file a"},` +
+		`{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_01_CmdsQ2CAdkIsdARwuwAB4684","content":"file b"},` +
+		`{"type":"text","text":"<system-reminder>both ran</system-reminder>"}]}]}`
+	if code, out := post(t, "/v1/messages", body); code != 200 {
+		t.Fatalf("status %d: %s", code, out)
+	}
+	if !strings.Contains(string(f.got), `"tool_use_id":"call_01_CmdsQ2CAdkIsdARwuwAB4684"`) {
+		t.Fatalf("the second call's result never reached the upstream: %s", f.got)
+	}
+}
+
+// The same split on the translated path: a request built for DeepSeek's
+// Messages endpoint is asked in sequential exchanges, one call each.
+func TestTranslatedMessagesParallelToolUsesSplitForDeepSeek(t *testing.T) {
+	r := &Request{Messages: []Message{
+		{Role: "user", Parts: []Part{{Kind: Text, Text: "run them both"}}},
+		{Role: "assistant", Parts: []Part{call("A"), call("B")}},
+		{Role: "user", Parts: []Part{result("B", "second"), result("A", "first"), {Kind: Text, Text: "done"}}},
+	}}
+	body, err := build(provider.Anthropic, r, "deepseek-flash", "api.deepseek.com", false)
+	if err != nil {
+		t.Fatal(err)
+	}
+	trace, msgs := traceOfMessages(t, body)
+	if want := "user assistant(A) user[A] assistant(B) user[B]"; trace != want {
+		t.Fatalf("trace = %q, want %q", trace, want)
+	}
+	if !anthropicValid(msgs) {
+		t.Fatal("sequence fails Anthropic's tool exchange validation")
+	}
+}
+
+// The split itself, on the body a Messages client sent: results out of
+// order and text beside them, an exchange of one call, and one whose
+// results don't answer every call, all as splitParallelToolUses leaves or
+// mends them for DeepSeek's Anthropic-format API (#4945).
+func TestSplitParallelToolUsesOnARelayedBody(t *testing.T) {
+	parallel := `{"model":"deepseek-flash","messages":[` +
+		`{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"run them both"}]},` +
+		`{"role":"assistant","content":[` +
+		`{"type":"text","text":"two calls"},` +
+		`{"type":"tool_use","id":"A","name":"run","input":{}},` +
+		`{"type":"tool_use","id":"B","name":"run","input":{}}]},` +
+		`{"role":"user","content":[` +
+		`{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"B","content":"second"},` +
+		`{"type":"text","text":"both ran"},` +
+		`{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"A","content":"first"}]}]}`
+	body := splitParallelToolUses([]byte(parallel))
+	trace, msgs := traceOfMessages(t, body)
+	if want := "user assistant(A) user[A] assistant(B) user[B]"; trace != want {
+		t.Fatalf("trace = %q, want %q", trace, want)
+	}
+	if !anthropicValid(msgs) {
+		t.Fatal("sequence fails Anthropic's tool exchange validation")
+	}
+	if got := msgs[1].Content[0].Text; got != "two calls" {
+		t.Fatalf("the assistant's words didn't stay with its first call: %q", got)
+	}
+	last := msgs[4].Content
+	if last[0].ToolUseID != "B" || string(last[0].Content) != `"second"` || last[1].Text != "both ran" {
+		t.Fatalf("the second result and what the user said are not together after it: %+v", last)
+	}
+	// an exchange of one call, and a body with no calls, come back as they were
+	for name, b := range map[string]string{
+		"one call":   `{"model":"m","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"run"},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"A","name":"run","input":{}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"A","content":"ok"}]}]}`,
+		"no calls":   `{"model":"m","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hi"},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"hello"}]}]}`,
+		"unanswered": `{"model":"m","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"run"},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"A","name":"run","input":{}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"B","name":"run","input":{}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"A","content":"ok"}]}]}`,
+	} {
+		if got := string(splitParallelToolUses([]byte(b))); got != b {
+			t.Fatalf("%s: a body with nothing to split changed: %s", name, got)
+		}
+	}
+}
diff --git a/internal/gateway/gateway.go b/internal/gateway/gateway.go
index 5feb16bbe..1f29f4334 100644
